---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Demo from "../../../components/Demo.astro";
import { buttonLinks, buttonActions, buttonSizes } from "../../../examples/buttons";
import { url } from "../../../lib/urls";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Buttons"
  description="Compact controls, clear hierarchy, and native semantics. The element does the work; the class supplies the presentation."
  section="Components"
  source="website/src/pages/docs/components/buttons.astro"
>
  <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
    <h2 id="links-and-actions">Navigation is a link. An action is a button.</h2>
    <p>
      Use <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> to navigate and
      <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> to perform an action. Both can use
      <code>lf-button</code>. A styled anchor still behaves like a link: readers can open it in a
      new tab, copy its address, and follow it without JavaScript.
    </p>
  </div>
  <Demo id="button-links" title="Navigation with a visual hierarchy" code={buttonLinks} />
  <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
    <p>
      Use <code>--solid</code> for a primary action, the unmodified button for a secondary action, and
      <code>--quiet</code> for a lower-emphasis option. Avoid making every action look primary.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <h2 id="native-actions">Native actions</h2>
      <p>
        Change the sample name, check its native validation, or reset it.
        <code>--danger</code> communicates a destructive action, but does not add a confirmation dialog
        or implement deletion. The discard action here only resets this local example.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Demo id="button-actions" title="Submit and reset behavior" code={buttonActions} />
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <p>
        The documentation’s <code>data-demo-form</code> handler prevents submission and reports a local
        result. It is not part of Lineframe. Without JavaScript, submitting reloads this example with
        sample values in the URL. Your application should supply its own form action and result handling.
      </p>
      <p>
        Always specify <code>type</code>. A button inside a form defaults to
        <code>submit</code>; use <code>type="button"</code> for unrelated actions and <code
          >type="reset"</code
        > only when reverting fields is intentional.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <h2 id="sizes-and-disabled">Sizes and the disabled state</h2>
      <p>
        <code>--small</code>, the default size, and <code>--large</code> provide three degrees of density.
        These size examples all lead to the icon guide. The unavailable action is a genuinely disabled
        native button.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Demo id="button-sizes" title="Three sizes and a disabled button" code={buttonSizes} />
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <p>
        Prefer the native <code>disabled</code> attribute on a button. It blocks activation and removes
        the button from the tab order. Explain elsewhere why an important action is unavailable.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Neither CSS nor <code>aria-disabled="true"</code> disables an anchor.</strong>
        If navigation is unavailable, render explanatory noninteractive text or implement the complete
        disabled behavior yourself. Do not leave a link that appears disabled but still navigates.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
    <h2 id="button-checklist">Before shipping</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Use an action-specific name: “Download report,” not “Click here.”</li>
      <li>Give icon-only controls an accessible name; hide decorative SVGs.</li>
      <li>Check focus visibility, wrapping, and all states in both themes.</li>
      <li>
        Prefer a 44px comfortable target. WCAG 2.2 AA’s target-size criterion uses 24px with
        exceptions; “small” does not remove that responsibility.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Continue with <a href={url("docs/icons/")}>icons</a> or
      <a href={url("docs/components/forms/")}>complete form patterns</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
